	abstract = {Plant factories significantly enhance production efficiency and resource utilization, yet their heterogeneous environments—comprising repetitive greenhouses and unstructured open-field farms—pose challenges for autonomous robotic operations. To address mapping and localization drift caused by severe perceptual aliasing, scene transitions, and dynamic disturbances, this article proposes multisensor adaptive fusion simultaneous localization and mapping (MSAF-SLAM), a novel adaptive tightly-coupled SLAM framework driven by deep reinforcement learning. The framework innovatively unifies an actor-critic-based twin delayed deep deterministic policy gradient decision mechanism with a factor graph optimization back-end, establishing a closed-loop “perception-decision-feedback-learning” paradigm. Through an experience-driven adaptive fusion strategy, the system dynamically optimizes multisensor fusion contributions based on environmental feedback, effectively mitigating the impact of nonlinear disturbances on state estimation. Evaluations were conducted using a developed agricultural robotic system across public datasets and real-world agricultural scenarios. Experimental results demonstrate that, compared with state-of-the-art static fusion methods, MSAF-SLAM significantly improves localization robustness and accuracy while exhibiting superior generalization capability and computational efficiency, meeting the practical deployment requirements of plant factories. © 2005-2012 IEEE.},
