	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045696865?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {This paper explores models and a methodology for decentralized control of a group of collaborative robot manipulators operating within a single working area with a human operator. This subject matter is pertinent within the broader context of the development of intelligent production systems of Industry 5.0. The proposed approach is predicated on the principles of multi-agent systems, wherein each robot functions as an autonomous agent, utilizing local information to make decisions and coordinate with other agents. The decentralized control model is constructed with safety parameters, trajectory optimization, and minimization of task completion time in the presence of a human operator as integral design elements. Control barrier functions and adaptive mechanisms for altering the trajectory when dynamic obstacles emerge are employed to ensure smooth movements and avert collisions. Numerical simulations have demonstrated the efficacy of the proposed method, as evidenced by the maintenance of an average distance between robots and humans of at least 0.35 m and a deviation from the optimal trajectory of no more than 6.5%. The present study demonstrated a 12% reduction in the execution time of manipulation operations in comparison with centralized control algorithms, while ensuring the preservation of safe interaction zones. The utilization of a decentralized approach exhibited high scalability and system resilience to individual agent failures. The proposed results confirm the feasibility of applying this method in the context of flexible production processes and human-robot collaboration. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.},
