	abstract = {Multi-Robot Task Allocation (MRTA) plays a crucial role in controlling autonomous robots in different practical domains, such as industrial automation, search and rescue, and self-driving vehicles. In this paper, a comprehensive review of MRTA techniques is done that considers the important challenges of scalability, dynamic environments, and uncertainty. It subdivides the existing solutions into learning-based, optimization-based, auction-based, and decentralized systems and examines their pros and cons in various scenarios. The paper also reviews hybrid strategies that have been used to enhance efficiency and flexibility by integrating both centralized and decentralized solutions. Additionally, theoretical backgrounds like queueing theory, planning algorithms, and game theory are mentioned because they have contributed to task allocation. Furthermore, novel technologies like large language models (LLMs) and reinforcement learning (RL) are considered in the context of supporting real-time decision-making in changing conditions. It also provides a comparative analysis of different MRTA, such as genetic algorithms (GA), the use of an auction algorithm and optimization algorithms, analyzing how these algorithms perform in different areas of application. Lastly, the paper presents future research directions aimed at enhancing the flexibility, stability, and scalability of MRTA frameworks which could support more effective coordination of multi-robots in increasingly complex and uncertain environments. © 2026 Elsevier Inc.},
