	abstract = {High-speed magnetic levitation (Maglev) trains demand exceptionally robust safety systems for accurate localization and obstacle detection, particularly under dynamic conditions, environmental disturbances, and potential sensor failures. Conventional signal-sensor systems are often unreliable due to inherent limitations, such as global navigation satellite system (GNSS) signal blockage in tunnels or the vulnerability of visual cameras to adverse lighting and motion blur. This paper proposes a novel multisensor fusion framework that integrates complementary suites of sensors, light detection and ranging, radio detection and ranging, event-based vision, and mid-wave infrared (MWIR) thermal imaging, along with GNSS and high-definition map data, for comprehensive situational awareness. By employing a confidence-based Kalman filter and an innovative health monitoring system, our framework ensures enhanced redundancy, accuracy, and reliability. We validate this system through realistic MATLAB simulations featuring a Japanese L0 series SCMaglev train responding to an earthquake-triggered hazard. The results demonstrate significant improvements in object tracking and localization precision, as quantified. The integrated health monitoring module effectively identifies sensor anomalies and triggers proactive maintenance alerts, laying the groundwork for a robust predictive maintenance strategy. Critically, while the system validated real-time obstacle detection at 650 m, simulations highlighted the physical challenges of stopping distances at extreme speeds, underscoring the need for supplementary infrastructure-based warning systems. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd},
