	abstract = {Human–robot collaboration is increasingly explored for its potential in real-world deployment under challenging conditions. Accurate and robust localization is essential for enabling such collaboration, particularly in infrastructure-free and degraded environments. This article presents an inertial collaborative simultaneous localization and mapping (SLAM) framework for teams comprising humans and legged robots. Each agent independently performs inertial odometry using a single inertial measurement unit (IMU), and the resulting motion vectors are integrated into the proposed SLAM system. A global grid map is estimated together with human–robot poses using a Rao–Blackwellized particle filter (RBPF) framework, whose particle sampling and weighting models are formulated specifically for the inertial-only setting. To mitigate long-term drift and maintain global consistency, keyframes are extracted and associated using spatial and magnetic cues for proprioceptive collaboration, and the resulting constraints are incorporated into a factor graph for joint optimization. The proposed system presents a collaborative SLAM framework that is lightweight and does not rely on conventional external environmental features. Two real-world experiments involving a Unitree B1 robot and two human participants demonstrate that the proposed method significantly outperforms conventional inertial odometry and collaborative SLAM baselines in localization accuracy and robustness. © 1963-2012 IEEE.},
