	abstract = {Loop closure detection (LCD) and global consistency constraint (GCC) constitute critical components in light detection and ranging (LiDAR)–inertial measurement unit (IMU) simultaneous localization and mapping (SLAM) systems. Neglecting the spatial uncertainty propagating from measurement uncertainty into point clouds can significantly impair both LCD and GCC robustness, ultimately degrading pose estimation accuracy in complex environments. To address this limitation, we propose measurement uncertainty-driven SLAM (MUD-SLAM), a probabilistic constraint graph framework that explicitly models measurement uncertainty distributions for covariance-aware loop closing. Specifically, using plane-fitting as a reference, we first establish a polynomial regression model for range measurement error distributions correlated with incidence angles. This model enables rigorous derivation of point cloud spatial uncertainty through error propagation. Hereby, a reliable LCD (RLCD) method is proposed, which integrates a robust K–D tree algorithm based on probabilistic geometric–semantic fusion for filtering loop candidate frames and an IMU-assisted geometric verification strategy to actively suppress false registration in feature degradation scenarios. Furthermore, an adaptive GCC (AGCC) model is constructed by the covariance-aware weighted strategy to achieve resilient optimization of loop closure. Comprehensive evaluations on complex and deteriorated environment datasets demonstrate MUD-SLAM’s superior accuracy and timeliness in bidirectional loops. Especially in adverse weather, about 21.61%, 15.71%, and 6.72% improvements of 3-D positioning accuracy are achieved in MUD-SLAM compared to LOAM, LEGO-LOAM, and LIO-SAM. MUD-SLAM provides new insights into enhancing the robustness against the degradations of the environment and sensors. © 1963-2012 IEEE.},
