	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045291180?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Robust simultaneous localization and mapping (SLAM) in dynamic environments constitutes a fundamental challenge for high-precision state estimation and mobile measurement systems. The conventional systems frequently fail in such scenarios due to the measurement uncertainty introduced by moving objects. While recent dynamic SLAM (DynaSLAM) methods attempt to filter these outliers, they often face two coupled bottlenecks: the erroneous rejection of valid static background features caused by coarse bounding-box filtering and the system-level computational overhead introduced by semantic perception, mask refinement, and dynamic feature filtering, which complicates deployment on resource-constrained embedded sensing platforms. To overcome these limitations, we propose D2-Lite SLAM, an efficient dynamic visual SLAM system via depth-aware masking and distilled lightweight detection transformer (DETR) with improved matching for fast convergence (DEIM). First, we introduce a depth-aware mask generation algorithm that uses local depth consistency to refine coarse detection boxes into compact pseudo-masks, thereby preserving valid static measurement features inside dynamic-object regions and alleviating feature overexcision. Second, to ensure real-time operation, we design a lightweight backbone for the DEIM module utilizing FasterBlock and inverted residual mobile block (iRMB) structures. This optimization significantly reduces computational complexity from 7.1 giga floating-point operations (GFLOPs) to 5.6 GFLOPs. Additionally, a progressive knowledge distillation strategy is implemented to transfer rich semantic representations from a complex teacher network, effectively compensating for the accuracy loss in the lightweight architecture. Extensive experiments on the TUM RGB-D and BONN datasets demonstrate that D2-Lite SLAM outperforms the state-of-the-art methods. The proposed approach achieves superior estimation accuracy and robustness while substantially lowering the computational load, maintaining a stable frame rate suitable for real-time on-device state estimation. © 1963-2012 IEEE.},
