	abstract = {This paper addresses the problem of coordinating multiple robots to enclose and track an unknown target under hybrid attacks. To ensure accurate localization and stable formation, a secure relative localization and formation control framework is proposed. First, a robust recursive least squares estimator (RLSE) is developed that explicitly models both denial of service and false data injection attacks in the estimation process. Second, a coupled-oscillator mechanism is designed to satisfy the persistent excitation condition and generate adaptive circular formation trajectories that follow the moving target. Third, a scalable distributed controller is formulated to maintain the enclosing formation and allow dynamic adjustment of the team size according to task demands. Simulation and experiment results show that, under representative hybrid attack scenarios, the proposed RLSE reduces the tracking RMSE from 0.85m to 0.15m and guarantees uniform ultimate boundedness with a 0.20m bound. Compared with the existing methods, the proposed approach decreases the tracking RMSE by 40 % and improves relative localization accuracy by 27 % in adversarial environments. These results validate that the proposed framework significantly enhances estimation accuracy and formation stability against realistic hybrid attacks. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
