	doi = {10.1016/j.neucom.2026.133593},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036448108?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Robustness under visual degradation remains a critical bottleneck for monocular SLAM, as environmental noise, motion blur, and illumination variation often lead to unstable feature tracking and trajectory drift. In this paper, we present HELF-SLAM, a monocular SLAM system built upon a hybrid-enhanced learning framework for distortion-resilient localization. At its core is HARMONY, a Hybrid Adaptive Robust Multi-scale keypoint extraction pipeline that bridges low-level image restoration and high-level geometric perception. Specifically, we propose RDFeat, a Restoration-guided Distortion-robust network that leverages an internal restoration prior to separate stable structural cues from degradation artifacts. To facilitate the integration of learned features into a sparse SLAM pipeline, we further introduce Adaptive Dynamic Threshold Relaxation (ADTR) to regulate feature density and spatial distribution, together with Softmax-based Localization (SBL) for sub-pixel keypoint refinement. These components make HARMONY a plug-and-play front-end that can be integrated into tracking, mapping, loop closing, and relocalization with minimal changes to the underlying SLAM architecture. Extensive evaluations on the TUM RGB-D, EuRoC, KITTI, and real-world MCD benchmarks show that HARMONY improves feature robustness, tracking stability, and trajectory accuracy under both synthetic perturbations and naturally degraded conditions. In the same monocular vision-only setting, HELF-SLAM achieves more robust trajectory estimation than representative classical, learned-feature, and deep odometry baselines while maintaining real-time performance. The source code is available at https://github.com/The1912/HELF-SLAM. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.},
