	doi = {10.1088/2631-8695/ae5b43},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036232789?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {To address the non-stationarity of sensor noise caused by factors such as uneven lighting and slippery ground during the movement of a cable joint robot to the joint operation point in a tunnel, the traditional Extended Kalman Filter (EKF) uses a fixed noise covariance parameter, which makes it difficult to maintain the high-precision state estimation required for the closed-loop control of the cable joint robot walking mechanism. To address this, this paper proposes an Adaptive Improved Extended Kalman Filter (A-IEKF) algorithm. By fusing the Sage-Husa online estimator with a fuzzy inference mechanism, it achieves coordinated dynamic adjustment of the process noise covariance matrix and the observation noise covariance matrix. Based on the high-precision pose estimation output of this filter, a sliding mode controller is further designed to realize trajectory tracking of the cable joint robot as it moves towards the target joint position. Experiments show that the root mean square value of the absolute trajectory error of A-IEKF on a self-built tunnel simulation platform is 0.016 m, which is about 80% lower than that of the traditional EKF (0.081 m); the median error of the heading angle estimation in the curve section is 0.61°, which is about 84% lower than that of the traditional EKF (3.76°). Under sudden lidar obstruction, the state estimation disturbance recovery convergence time is 0.21 s, which is about 74% shorter than the traditional EKF (0.82 s). Under sideslip conditions, the root mean square error of velocity command tracking is 0.034 m s<sup>−1</sup>, which is about 52% lower than the traditional EKF (0.071 m s<sup>−1</sup>). This research provides an effective technical path to improve the positioning robustness and closed-loop control accuracy of cable joint robots in complex environments. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved.},
