	abstract = {While hybrid indoor localization seeks to combine the strengths of wireless and visual sensing, existing systems remain vulnerable to signal multi-path effects and the temporal brittleness of visual landmarks caused by daily environmental dynamics. This paper proposes a robust, hierarchical indoor positioning framework that achieves long-term stability through deep cross-modal integration. We first introduce an adaptive reliability arbitration mechanism that dynamically weights wireless and visual modalities based on real-time signal stability metrics, preventing error propagation during sensor degradation. To address the challenge of environmental drift, we develop a Semantically-Informed Portability Masking strategy. This method utilizes a category-specific masking protocol to prioritize stable structural features over transient objects (e.g., personal items) to construct a resilient, fine-grained image fingerprint database managed by a constrained ball-tree architecture. Unique to this work is an extensive longitudinal evaluation: we demonstrate that the system maintains sub-0.4-meter accuracy on datasets collected six months post-training, effectively overcoming significant object-category drift. Under controlled conditions, the mean distance error is reduced to under 0.3 meters during free movement and 0.025 meters for stationary users. Furthermore, stress tests involving adversarial image injection and network packet loss confirm the system's resilience, with errors capped at 0.7 meters via particle filter fallback. Our results indicate that this hierarchical approach provides a scalable, device-agnostic, and low-overhead solution for high-precision mobile computing applications in evolving real-world environments. © 2002-2012 IEEE.},
