	abstract = {This paper presents a robust localization method for the Clearpath Husky A200 robot that leverages a Dualframe Extended Kalman Filter (DFEKF) to fuse data from GPS, an IMU, and wheel odometry, implemented using the Robot Operating System (ROS). The proposed architecture employs two concurrent Extended Kalman Filter instances operating in different reference frames: a local filter that fuses wheel odometry and IMU data to provide continuous pose estimation in the odometry frame, and a global filter that incorporates GPS observations via the navsat_transform_node to correct longterm drift and maintain consistency in the map frame. The method is validated through both Gazebo simulations and realworld field experiments, with ground-truth provided by an Xsens Vision Navigator system. The simulation results indicate that sensor fusion reduces the average positional error to 0.044 m(x) and 0.105 m(y), significantly outperforming standalone odometry, which exhibits deviations of 329.31 m (x) and 689.65 m (y). In real-world experiments with loop-closure, EKF-based fusion achieved a combined error of 1.89 m compared to 187.55 m for odometry alone, while an intermediate configuration using only odometry and IMU reduced the error to 3.48 m. These findings highlight the effectiveness of multi-sensor fusion for robust, accurate localization across diverse motion scenarios, and demonstrate the practical viability of the dualframe architecture for real-time deployment on skid-steered mobile robotic platforms operating in GPS-constrained outdoor environments. © 2026 IEEE.},
