	abstract = {The accuracy of LiDAR-IMU extrinsic calibration critically affects the performance of LiDAR-inertial simultaneous localization and mapping systems operating in confined underground spaces. However, existing methods typically rely on feature-rich or tightly controlled environments, which are difficult to guarantee in real underground operations. Consequently, they are insufficient for handling extrinsic drift and enabling timely recalibration in practical underground settings, where mechanical vibrations or collisions frequently occur. To address these challenges, an enhanced LiDAR-IMU calibration system, termed DD_Calib, featuring dual-modal LiDAR odometry and dual-domain synchronization is proposed. Firstly, the proposed method improves LiDAR odometry update frequency through an adaptive frame segmentation strategy guided by degeneration awareness. It further enhances pose accuracy and robustness by constructing dual-modal constraints from geometric and intensity features. In addition, a dual-domain temporal offset estimation method compensates for timing discrepancies between the LiDAR and IMU, ensuring consistent temporal alignment. Finally, a unified optimization model is established with acceleration and angular velocity residuals to estimate both rotational and translational extrinsic parameters. Extensive calibration experiments across structured, semi-structured, and unstructured underground environments demonstrate that DD_Calib outperforms state-of-the-art methods in terms of both accuracy and robustness. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved.},
	type = {Article},
	publication_stage = {Final},
