	abstract = {Advancing self-driving car research often requires expensive full-scale prototypes, limiting accessibility for researchers and small-scale developers. Simulation environments and toy models often lack the fidelity needed to assess real-world autonomous driving behavior. This work presents a low-cost, modular self-driving car platform designed to bridge this gap by enabling outdoor testing with over-the-counter components. The developed platform is built on a toddler car chassis and features a modular architecture with essential plug-and-play sensors and a swappable control unit. It adopts a Bring Your Own Device (BYOD) approach, allowing users to integrate additional sensing and computation units, such as smartphones and portable notebook computers, while keeping costs low. Our experiments focus on evaluating the localization performance of the platform, where robust localization was achieved under challenging conditions using an Extended Kalman Filter (EKF) to fuse wheel odometry, IMU, and GNSS data. We validated our approach through real-world tests, achieving a lowest average localization error of 3.45 m on a 350 m track. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
