	abstract = {Accurate localization for mobile robots remains challenging under time-varying sensor uncertainty, where individual sensing modalities exhibit complementary but imperfect characteristics. Conventional filtering or learning-based approaches often struggle to maintain robustness when measurement quality varies over time. To address this issue, this paper proposes a hybrid multi-sensor fusion framework that combines learning-based error compensation with model-based state estimation. The method integrates LSTM-based correction of position observations with a dual-layer Multiple Model Adaptive Estimation (MMAE) structure and a UKF-based refinement mechanism, enabling adaptive handling of varying uncertainty conditions. Simulation results in a Gazebo environment show that the proposed approach consistently improves localization accuracy compared with standalone UKF and LSTM methods across multiple noise levels, achieving RMSE reductions ranging from 8.8% to 49.6%. The performance gain becomes more pronounced under moderate and severe noise conditions, where single-model approaches are less effective. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.},
