	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/JSEN.2026.3718943},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048538908?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Reliable real-time fault diagnosis of onboard sensors is essential for UAV navigation and localization in safety-critical missions. Although recent fault detection and diagnosis (FDD) methods have improved robustness to class imbalance and limited fault data, incipient and non-catastrophic faults under asynchronous multi-rate sensing and onboard resource constraints remain insufficiently addressed. In this work, we formulate a real-time UAV sensor fault diagnosis problem under mission-level navigation performance constraints, where the operational regime is defined by mission-level navigation error criteria rather than catastrophic mission failure. To address this problem, we propose MR-IGATNet, a multi-rate multi-stream diagnosis network that combines InceptionTime window encoding with graph attention based fusion for asynchronous multi-sensor data. MR-IGATNet adopts a group-wise architecture aligned with feature semantics and sensor update frequencies. It further introduces a dual-level fusion module conditioned on asynchronous update intervals (Δt), combining graph-attention message passing with query-based pooling (GAT-QP) to integrate information across feature groups and sensor streams. Extensive offline and hardware-in-the-loop (HIL) evaluations show consistent improvements over strong baselines, while real-time deployment results confirm 9.98 ms latency suitable for onboard diagnosis. © 2001-2012 IEEE.},
