	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105031485800?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {In complex indoor positioning environments, wireless positioning systems are frequently impaired by non-line-of-sight (NLOS) propagation and multipath effects, which significantly degrade the accuracy of ranging measurements and consequently compromise positioning performance. These challenges are particularly pronounced in systems utilizing low-cost measurement hardware. To address this issue, this article proposes an adaptive fusion localization method based on wireless multisensor ranging signal loss. First, an ultrawideband (UWB) signal free-space path loss model combined with Kalman filtering is employed to dynamically adjust the observation noise covariance, enabling real-time adaptive noise suppression for smoother and more accurate ranging. Second, inertial measurement unit (IMU) data are fused with UWB measurements using adaptive noise characteristics derived from UWB filtering. To balance sensor reliability, trust weights are assigned based on timestamped noise variance, prioritizing short-term IMU credibility while constraining UWB trust thresholds to prevent fusion bias. A scalable simulation platform emulating real-world NLOS scenarios validates the method, incorporating vehicle models and multisensor interactions. The simulation results demonstrate that adaptive filtering reduces ranging fluctuations at each base station to varying degrees compared to actual UWB accuracy. Furthermore, the fusion approach achieves positioning accuracy improvements of 47.66% and 30.92% over standard Kalman and extended Kalman filter (EKF) methods, respectively. Real-world tests confirm robustness in complex indoor environments.  © 1963-2012 IEEE.},
