	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045918493?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Accurate obstacle ranging is essential for haul-truck safety in open-pit mines, where dust, illumination changes, weak textures, and large target-scale variation degrade detection and stereo matching. This study presents an in-domain offline detection-guided stereo framework for 2.5-D obstacle localization. It couples Detection-Informed Stereo YOLO (DIS-YOLO), a lightweight YOLOv11n-based detector, with a customized Semi-Global Block Matching (SGBM) variant that uses detections to restrict regions of interest, adapt disparity-search parameters, fuse Census/color/gradient costs, and refine valid disparities at object measurement points. Experiments were mainly conducted using recorded stereo data from one primary open-pit mine, with an additional small external-mine subset used for limited transfer validation. DIS-YOLO achieved 92.1% mAP@0.5. Across 20 test distances from 5 to 100 m, the proposed pipeline reduced mean absolute error (MAE) from 1.943 to 1.006 m relative to original SGBM. On 240 paired valid-output real-scene measurements, MAE decreased from 1.47 to 0.80 m, a 45.8% relative reduction with a bootstrap 95% confidence interval of 41.9%–49.6%. The distance-wise uncertainty budget yielded standard uncertainties of 0.04–1.31 m and expanded uncertainties of 0.07–2.63 m (k = 2). The results support in-domain offline validation and preliminary external-mine transfer evidence; broader cross-site, cross-camera, underground, and closed-loop deployment still require larger multi-site validation. © 2026 The Authors.},
