	doi = {10.1109/TASE.2026.3702564},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041957016?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {This paper addresses a variant of the conflict-based search (CBS) algorithm for multi-agent path finding, presenting an effective cluster reasoning heuristic that searches for and detects conflicts involving more than two agents. The proposed in-depth cluster reasoning technique systematically alters the reference agent when computing the heuristic cost-to-go, and this yields a tighter estimate. It then balances the complexity of expanding CBS nodes against that of locating clusters by systematically scheduling the associated parameters. Numerical experiments demonstrate that the proposed algorithm successfully detects clusters that state-of-the-art algorithms cannot identify and produces solutions for various extreme scenarios that existing algorithms cannot solve. Note to Practitioners - Multi-agent path finding (MAPF) computes collision-free routes for many agents sharing a map, and underpins applications such as automated warehouses, vehicle routing, and other coordinated robotic fleets. In practice, the difficulty grows sharply with the number of agents and the complexity of the environment, so conventional optimal solvers frequently exceed acceptable time limits. This work targets exactly those hard instances, producing high-quality routes even where prior methods return no solution in time. For practitioners, the method extends the range of problems that can be solved with a guaranteed-optimal solution rather than an approximate one. It is also tunable on a per-instance basis: parameters can be matched to the characteristics of a given site - for example, agent density or graph layout - so that performance on a specific deployment exceeds what fixed, general-purpose settings achieve. This makes it suitable as a configurable planning component that operators can adapt to their own facility. The grid-based routes the algorithm produces are a practical foundation for real robotic systems. Holonomic robots that move directly on the grid can follow them without modification. Robots with kinematic constraints can treat each route as a reference and adapt it: as shown in our experiments, the discrete routes can be smoothed into dynamically feasible trajectories that remain collision-free under positional noise. A current limitation is that the approach addresses the initial planning stage only; online re-planning during execution and full hardware integration require further development before field deployment.  © 2004-2012 IEEE.},
