	abstract = {Accurate and robust moving object detection and tracking are critical measurement tasks for autonomous vehicles operating in complex dynamic scenes. Cooperative perception, leveraging multi-vehicle information sharing, offers a way to overcome inherent limitations of single-vehicle measurement systems. This paper presents a cooperative measurement solution based on LiDAR point clouds and inertial measurement unit (IMU), comprising a cooperative object detection module using temporally fused point clouds and an interactive multi-model (IMM)-SCIF-JPDA based multi-object tracker. In the detection module, temporal point cloud information is integrated via IMU mechanization, and an adaptive feature-level fusion model is applied to improve detection precision while maintaining robustness under occlusion and sparse point cloud conditions. For the tracking module, an IMM filter combined with the split covariance intersection filter is developed to handle correlation problems and measurement uncertainty arising from unknown object motion patterns. This module is further integrated with joint probability data association to enhance tracking stability and accuracy. Comparative experimental results are provided to quantify the performance of the proposed cooperative perception solution, with detailed analysis of its improvements in both accuracy and robustness. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved. This article is available under the terms of the IOP-Standard License.},
