	abstract = {To address the limitations of the feature extraction in LeGO-LOAM and the problems encountered in ground segmentation within indoor environments, this paper proposes an improved LeGO-LOAM algorithm that incorporates adaptive feature extraction and enhanced ground segmentation. The algorithm performs edge detection through normal vector estimation of points, determines the neighborhood size based on the point cloud density within a specified radius around the current point, and calculates smoothness and curvature. In the ground segmentation process, an uncertainty metric is introduced to emphasize the impact of height on ground point recognition as the horizontal distance increases. Subsequently, validation was performed using the KITTI dataset. The results show that compared with other SLAM algorithms, our algorithm achieves higher accuracy on the 00, 02, and 05 sequences of the KITTI dataset. Actual experiments were conducted in an indoor storage environment. The results indicate that the improved method proposed in this paper not only achieves a higher degree of matching with the real environment during map construction, but also realizes more stable poses along the y-axis, with the trough value increasing from −0.85 in LeGO-LOAM to −0.14. © 2025 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved.},
