	doi = {10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3713316},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045244426?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Reliable trajectory tracking of quadrotor unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) becomes particularly challenging when actuator degradation and corrupted state measurements occur simultaneously, because input-channel compensation alone cannot prevent faulty feedback from degrading the closed-loop performance. This paper investigates the trajectory tracking control problem of quadrotor UAVs subject to model uncertainties, external disturbances, actuator faults, and gain-bias faults in the fused pose feedback channels. To address these coupled effects, a two-layer observer-controller framework is proposed. In the measurement layer, the gain-bias faults in the fused pose feedback are rewritten as equivalent measurement-channel faults, and a kinematics-assisted measurement-channel reconstructor is introduced to recover the position and attitude signals used by the controller. In the control layer, actuator faults, model uncertainties, and external disturbances are cast into matched lumped disturbances and estimated by a fixed-time extended state observer (ESO). A smoothed-barrier fixed-time sliding mode scheme is then developed using the reconstructed states, and the ESO estimate is fed forward to compensate matched input-channel faults. The smoothed barrier gain strengthens the super-twisting action near a prescribed boundary, avoiding the gain drift of accumulated adaptive laws and reducing the need for a priori disturbance bounds in online implementation. A Lyapunov-based analysis shows that the observer errors, sliding variables, and reconstructed-state tracking errors are practically fixed-time bounded. The true tracking errors are further confined to residual neighborhoods determined by the measurement-channel reconstruction accuracy and the implementation smoothing. Numerical simulations under actuator faults, measurement-channel faults, and compound fault conditions are presented to evaluate the proposed method.  © 2026 The Authors.},
