	doi = {10.1016/j.measurement.2026.122172},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041498016?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Point cloud registration is a core technology procedure for environmental perception interaction and accurate pose estimation in intelligent mobile robot fields. Existing research primarily focuses on improving the performance of registration algorithms, but lacks the quantitative analysis and evaluation of theoretical accuracy, which limits its applicability in scenarios that demand high reliability. Based on the Cramér–Rao lower bound (CRLB) theory, this paper quantitatively analyzes the influence factors on point cloud registration error, including point cloud measurement noise, effective quantity and spatial distribution, The derived CRLB establishes a theoretical lower bound on the estimation variance of the registration pose, offering fundamental insights into the achievable precision under given sensing conditions. For LiDAR-IMU fusion odometry applications, an adaptive fusion strategy is proposed which dynamically adjusts the information fusion weights according to the CRLB of registration pose estimation. Subsequently, the strategy is implemented within enhances the accuracy and robustness of LiDAR-IMU odometry across different scenarios. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd},
