	volume = {75},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/TIM.2026.3671940},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105033645985?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Robust and accurate LiDAR odometry is crucial for enabling reliable navigation and mapping for autonomous robots in perceptually challenging environments, such as long corridors, tunnels, and open squares. This article proposes a novel geometry-intensity fused LiDAR-inertial odometry (LIO) framework that enables accurate localization in geometrically degenerate environments. Different from the existing methods, the key insight of the proposed method is that the redundancy-degeneracy-aware geometric measurements are fused with complementary intensity measurements to construct degeneracy-optimization constraints. This ensures that the system state is updated along the higher information directions, thus enhancing localization accuracy and system robustness. In particular, the novel geometric measurement model is proposed to minimize redundancy in scan-to-map matches and indicate nondegenerate directions for state optimization. In addition, the probabilistic adaptive voxel map is leveraged for more accurate geometric measurements and degeneracy detection. When the above geometric measurements and the complementary intensity measurements are both available, the joint update of the constrained iterative extended Kalman filter (CIEKF) is performed to obtain the state estimation. Comparative experiments on public datasets are conducted to demonstrate the superior performance of the proposed odometry in terms of localization accuracy and map consistency, particularly in degenerate environments, such as long tunnels and flat fields.  © 1963-2012 IEEE.},
