	abstract = {Robot scanning is a highly accurate and efficient method for measuring distributed features of large-scale components (LSCs). Nevertheless, current research about scanning path planning is mostly based on the theoretical model. This paper proposes a self-adaptive modification method for the robot scanning path of distributed features of LSCs based on pose estimation in case the scanning path planned based on the theoretical model cannot match the actual state. Firstly, incomplete point cloud data of LSC distributed features is obtained using the offline-planned robot scanning path due to the pose deviation between the theoretical model and the actual state of the LSC. Secondly, a pose estimation method based on segmentation, completion, and registration for the incomplete point cloud of the LSC is proposed to estimate its actual pose. In addition, this paper creatively proposes the Nominal Data Assisted Point Completion Network (NDAPCN) for point cloud completion and Sample Consensus Initial Alignment-Twice Iterative Closest Point (SACIA-TICP) for point cloud registration to achieve accurate pose estimation. Then, the theoretical scanning path is modified according to the estimated actual pose, through which the complete point cloud of distributed features can be obtained. Finally, the effectiveness of the proposed method is validated through simulation and experiments. The results demonstrate that the proposed method can ensure the integrity of the scanning results in cases where the actual state of the LSC is inconsistent with its theoretical model. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd},
