	abstract = {In this work, we present a novel combined path-planning methodology which fuses a boosted Jump Point Search (JPS) and a refined Dynamic Window Algorithm (DWA), with the aim of mitigating the drawbacks inherent in their respective conventional implementations. Specifically, the search efficiency of the traditional JPS method degrades significantly in large-scale grid maps. Furthermore, it tends to generate angular paths that are kinematically unsuitable for the platform In contrast, the conventional DWA method uses fixed evaluation function weights and tends to exhibit over-avoidance or insufficient speed in complex dynamic environments. global path planning module optimizes JPS Algorithm: Introduces a dynamic weighting mechanism into the heuristic function, designing directional weighting based on the angle between the current node, starting point, and destination. Sets directional weakening factors to optimize the search range. Eliminates redundant nodes through line segment-obstacle intersection detection. The local obstacle avoidance module improves the DWA algorithm: An adaptive weight adjustment mechanism dynamically optimizes evaluation function weights based on environmental complexity metrics such as obstacle density, path curvature, and action space ratio. Our experimental findings indicate that the proposed methodology markedly improves trajectory smoothness and real-time obstacle avoidance performance, thus rendering it well-suited for deployment in mobile robot navigation contexts. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
