	abstract = {Robot positioning covers a broad range of sensor types and sensing modalities and has inspired fabulous graph-simultaneous localization and mapping (SLAM) frameworks for multisource information fusion. This article is devoted to an indoor–outdoor transition-oriented navigation system design. The first step focuses on high-precision, globally referenced positioning information acquisition. An ultra wideband (UWB) solution that allows data collection in uncontrolled environments is conducted. And in this context, a computationally cheap, deep neural network (DNN) based range error compensation model is derived from the insights gained in the experiments. Data’s spatiotemporal alignment, caching, quality control, etc. cover different aspects of the measurement processing of our designed loosely-coupled navigation system. As one of our important contributions, a methodology to gain additional insights into the unification of indoor and outdoor coordinates is proposed. By employing a pose graph optimization framework, we compare our design against state-of-the-art methods on benchmark datasets and in real-world tests. Results reveal our design yields superior performance. On the real robot platform, it obtains a 32.99% accuracy improvement over the suboptimal baseline method. Statistical analysis confirms that all p-values for pairwise comparisons against three baseline models are less than 0.001, proving the statistical significance of the performance enhancement. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.},
