	abstract = {This paper investigates the particle filtering problem of mobile robots in distributed cyber-physical systems under the Round-Robin protocol when subjected to denial-of-service (DoS) attacks and bilateral measurement censoring (BMC). To account for the unequal contributions of different sensor nodes to state estimation, a mutual information entropy fusion method is introduced to dynamically adjust the fusion weight of each node. Considering the network communication congestion caused by data conflicts in the case of limited network transmission resources, this research proposes to schedule the data in nodes through the Round-Robin protocol in a predefined transmission sequence. To handle the problem of large errors in existing state estimation algorithms under DoS attacks and BMC, a modified likelihood function is constructed by fully considering the mathematical characteristics of DoS attacks and BMC, and a novel particle filter algorithm is designed. A trajectory tracking case of a mobile robot is used to verify the effectiveness of the proposed algorithm. The results show that compared with the basic particle filter, the proposed particle filter reduces the average RMSE by 55.35% in the x -direction and 69.57% in the y -direction. The proposed filtering algorithm can provide a reference for the state estimation of robots under interference. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.},
