	doi = {10.1109/TMRB.2025.3646762},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105026082268?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Transnasal surgical robots must operate within the confined anatomical space of the nasal cavity, which requires high precision in trajectory planning and real-time correction. This study presents a comprehensive trajectory planning and real-time correction framework for transnasal surgical robots, integrating Remote Center of Motion (RCM) constraints and real-time fiber optic sensing feedback. A personalized 3D point cloud and grid map of the nasal cavity are constructed based on computed tomography (CT) imaging. The improved RRT* algorithm is used to generate collision-free paths that adhere to RCM constraints. To address sensor measurement errors, an Adaptive Unscented Kalman Filter (AUKF) is employed to accurately estimate the end-effector’s position and correct minor deviations in real-time through position control. When significant trajectory deviation or contact with nasal tissue is detected, a local trajectory replanning strategy with multiple constraints is introduced, dynamically adjusting the path based on sensor feedback. Experimental results show that the proposed framework effectively generates anatomically compatible motion paths and provides real-time trajectory error correction in complex nasal environments, offering a reliable and precise solution for robot-assisted transnasal surgery. © 2018 IEEE.},
