	abstract = {Accurate and robust localization is essential for autonomous robotic systems operating in infrastructurelimited environments. Visual-inertial odometry (VIO) provides high short-term accuracy but suffers from cumulative drift, while ultrawideband (UWB) ranging offers absolute distance constraints yet requires multiple anchors or careful deployment. To address these limitations, this article presents SAMT-VIR, a single-anchor multitag visual- inertial-ranging (VIR) localization framework that achieves drift-bounded pose estimation with minimal environmental infrastructure. The proposed system tightly couples monocular vision, inertial sensing, and high-frequency multitag UWB ranging within a unified factor-graph optimization. Robot states are propagated to UWB measurement timestamps to fully exploit dense range observations; simultaneously, UWB tag-to-body extrinsics are explicitly modeled, and the anchor position is determined through optimization, thereby eliminating systematic ranging biases and enabling automatic anchor self-calibration. A UWB measurement validation mechanism, together with a relative transformation constraint is further introduced to enhance robustness under degraded UWB conditions. Extensive evaluations on public datasets and real-world indoor and outdoor experiments demonstrate that the proposed method consistently outperforms state-of-the-art VIO and VIR approaches in localization accuracy, drift suppression, and robustness, while maintaining real-time performance on embedded hardware. These results validate the effectiveness of multitag extrinsic-aware UWB fusion for reliable localization in practical robotic deployments.  © 2026 IEEE.},
