	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105040756420?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {This paper focuses on the problem of practical prescribed-time (PPT) trajectory tracking control for wheeled mobile robots (WMRs) operating under the influence of wheel slip. Initially, the dynamic model of the WMR incorporating both matched and mismatched disturbances is established. To address these disturbances, a robust PPT control framework is developed based on a dual-observer architecture, where two prescribed-time disturbance observers (PTDOs) are constructed for estimating and compensating for the respective disturbances. By incorporating the disturbance feedforward information from the PTDOs into the backstepping design, a prescribed-time filtering backstepping control (PTFBC) law is formulated. Theoretical analysis confirms the PPT stability of the closed-loop system, ensuring that both observation and tracking errors converge to a tunable neighborhood of the origin at the specified settling time. Finally, comprehensive simulation studies are performed to validate the effectiveness and performance advantages of the proposed control strategy. © 2026 Elsevier Inc.},
