	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105040549434?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Accurate localization is crucial for the safe navigation of autonomous vehicles, as it directly affects path planning and obstacle avoidance. However, global positioning system (GPS) and light detection and ranging (LiDAR) sensors, commonly used for position estimation, have inherent limitations: GPS is prone to signal loss or multipath effects in urban environments, while LiDAR can be impacted by adverse weather or reflective surfaces. These issues often introduce anomalies in the position data, leading to unreliable localization results. To address these challenges, we propose a robust localization framework combining a long short-term memory (LSTM)-Transformer autoencoder for anomaly detection with a multisensor Kalman filter (MSKF). The anomaly detection model dynamically assesses the reliability of GPS and LiDAR data, allowing the MSKF to adjust the measurement covariance based on sensor conditions. Experimental results on the KITTI dataset and real-world highway scenarios demonstrate that our method improves position accuracy and robustness against sensor anomalies.  © 1963-2012 IEEE.},
