	abstract = {RGB cameras depend on adequate illumination to capture rich environmental details, rendering simultaneous localization and mapping (SLAM) unreliable in extreme lighting conditions. Red-green-blue-depth cameras, however, inherently produce near-infrared (NIR) images alongside visible ones, providing spectral data beyond the human-visible range. To address this, we propose NIR-RGB-depth (NRD)-SLAM, a visual–inertial SLAM system that fuses NIR, RGB, and depth data for robust localization. To bridge dual modal gaps, we apply targeted enhancement and denoising to low-quality NIR images. Neural network modules handle feature extraction and matching, boosting accuracy across modalities. We also introduce a dynamic dual-threshold module to use modalities selectively, and design a dual-light visual odometry for effective tracking under varied lighting. In relocalization, the global descriptor is used to improve global consistency, enabling cross-modal loop detection. Moreover, we incorporate prior depth from the camera to dynamically optimize inverse depth residuals. This stabilizes monocular triangulation and reduces drift. Experiments show that, under normal lighting, NRD-SLAM cuts trajectory error by 5.7% and 2.6% relative to visual-inertial navigation system-Fusion and SuperPoint-Loop, respectively. Even in low light or complex loop scenarios, it maintains errors below 3%, demonstrating superior robustness. © 2005-2012 IEEE.},
