	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034710718?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Sewer infrastructure faces escalating threats from corrosion, fractures, and blockages caused by rapid urbanization, pipeline aging, and climate change. These failures pose serious risks to public health and the environment, which underscore the urgent need for advanced inspection technologies. This study provides a systematic review of robotic technologies for sewer inspection, synthesizing 183 key publications from 2011 to 2025. Our analysis identifies four defining trends and their associated bottlenecks: (1) multi-sensor fusion has become a cornerstone for enhancing inspection accuracy, yet real-time data processing remains a challenge; (2) hybrid localization techniques mitigate cumulative drift in signal-denied environments but require rigorous spatiotemporal synchronization; (3) intelligent control architectures improve stability under nonlinear pipeline dynamics, though their efficacy is tied to high-quality training data and computational overhead; and (4) data-driven digital twins enable predictive maintenance but remain constrained by data interoperability and communication reliability. To further advance the field, we propose research directions and advocate for the integration of Explainable AI (XAI) to enhance the robustness of robotic sewer inspection solutions. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.},
