	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045444008?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Robotic pose estimation in extreme industrial environments, such as aerospace assembly and deep-sea maintenance, is challenged by severe sensory degradation caused by low illumination, heavy occlusion, and acoustic reverberation. These factors degrade individual modalities and limit the effectiveness of conventional static fusion strategies. To address this issue, we present a reliability-aware visual–acoustic framework for robust pose inference under dynamically degraded conditions. The approach integrates multi-scale visual enhancement to preserve geometric details under noise, a coordinate-aware acoustic encoding mechanism to capture structured directional cues, and bidirectional cross-attention to align heterogeneous features through cross-modal evidence exchange. Building on this, a reliability-aware adaptive fusion module estimates modality confidence and dynamically regulates their contributions during inference. Experiments on a multimodal dataset achieve 92.9% pose estimation accuracy with 39 ms latency. Beyond overall accuracy, repeated-trial results show improved stability, while controlled degradation experiments demonstrate more graceful performance decay under adverse conditions. Analysis of failure cases further provides insight into the method’s limitations. Real-world grasping experiments validate its effectiveness and practical deployability. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.},
