	abstract = {This article investigates the problem of state estimation in target tracking systems under non-Gaussian noises. Conventional interacting multiple model (IMM) filters are prone to divergence in the presence of non-Gaussian process or measurement noises. To address this issue, a robust IMM Kalman filtering algorithm based on the generalized Gaussian-Cauchy mixture correntropy (GGCM-IMMKF) is proposed. Compared with the conventional single-Gaussian-kernel methods, the proposed approach provides greater flexibility in modeling a broader range of noise distributions. Furthermore, an adaptive optimization strategy is developed to determine the kernel parameters online, and an explicit lower bound for the kernel bandwidth is derived via outlier-ratio estimation. In addition, the convergence of the fixed-point iteration and the computational complexity of the GGCM-IMMKF are also analyzed. Finally, simulation and real-world experiments demonstrate that the proposed algorithm outperforms existing IMM-based filtering methods.  © 1965-2011 IEEE.},
