	abstract = {Low-cost micro inertial measurement units (MIMUs) are widely used for tilt angle (pitch and roll) measurement. However, under non-stationary state, their accuracy is degraded by gyroscope drift and by the influence of motion-induced acceleration on the accelerometer measurements. This paper proposes a Q-learning adaptive complementary filter (QACF) for robust tilt angle estimation using low-cost MIMUs. QACF constructs an attitude difference metric from the difference between short-term gyroscope-integrated attitude increments and complementary-filter attitude updates, which is used to indicate the reliability of accelerometer-based correction. A Q-learning policy then adjusts the complementary-filter gain online, enabling adaptive weighting between gyroscope propagation and accelerometer feedback under different motion states. Static, dynamic, and field experiments are conducted to validate the performance of QACF. The results show that, under both static and dynamic conditions with acceleration disturbances, the QACF algorithm achieves lower maximum absolute error, standard deviation, and mean error than the comparison algorithms, demonstrating favorable attitude estimation capability. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved. This article is available under the terms of the IOP-Standard License.},
