	doi = {10.1109/TASE.2026.3715807},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045717383?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {This paper introduces a novel risk-aware zone control barrier function (RAZ-CBF) to solve the integrated problem of safety and precision tracking for nonholonomic wheeled vehicle under input saturation. The control architecture designs a nominal tracking controller (via input-output linearization) with a sliding mode term to expedite tracking recovery when the safety filter diverts control input. This safety filter is realized through the proposed RAZ-CBF, which efficiently quantifies collision risk using a geometric representation and is resolved in real-time via a quadratic programming (QP) framework. Crucially, the novel RAZ-CBF captures the geometric relationship between relative motion and relative position. The control commands are further processed through a saturation function to ensure stability under physical actuation limits. Simulations confirm accurate tracking and agile avoidance under input saturation, showing deployability for real systems. Note to Practitioners—This work presents a practical control strategy for precise tracking and guaranteed safety of nonholonomic wheeled vehicles under actuator saturation and complex environments. The core of the approach is a co-design where a sliding-mode-enhanced tracking controller (based on input-output linearization) is seamlessly integrated with a novel RAZ-CBF via QP. The sliding mode term accelerates tracking convergence, compensating for transient deviations induced by the safety filter.This novel RAZ-CBF embeds relative velocity into the barrier function, resulting in a safety condition that directly reflects collision risk and reduces conservative behavior compared to distance-based approaches. Simulations validate accurate tracking and agile avoidance even under input saturation, demonstrating a deployable framework for real-world autonomous systems. © 2004-2012 IEEE.},
