	abstract = {Mobile agricultural sensing systems increasingly combine GNSS, IMU, LiDAR, camera, radar, encoder, and cropspecific sensors on tractors, ground robots, UAVs, sprayers, harvesters, and phenotyping platforms. These systems are usually discussed in terms of perception accuracy, navigation error, detection performance, or decision-support utility. However, the temporal assumptions that make heterogeneous sensor fusion meaningful are often less visible. A crop observation is only useful when its measured value, position, orientation, and actuation command correspond to the correct physical time. Small timing offsets can become spatial errors when the platform is moving, especially in row-crop navigation, orchard mapping, UAV georeferencing, real-Time spraying, and high-Throughput phenotyping. This paper provides a structured survey of temporal integrity in mobile agricultural sensing. Literature searches across IEEE Xplore, Scopus, Web of Science, ScienceDirect, and SpringerLink were used to identify representative studies and map how timestamping, synchronization, latency, multirate alignment, and timing-induced errors are reported. The paper contributes: (i) a temporal-integrity pipeline from acquisition to decision output; (ii) a taxonomy of clock, acquisition, communication, buffering, and alignment-related timing errors; (iii) a motion-Aware error framework linking timing offset to translational and rotational spatial error; and (iv) AgriTIME, a compact reporting checklist for agricultural mobile sensing studies. The review shows that timing is often reported indirectly through real-Time performance, frame rate, latency, or sensorfusion architecture, while explicit timestamp origin, matching tolerance, clock reference, and residual offset evaluation are less consistently reported. The paper argues that temporal integrity should be treated as a core design and reporting requirement for connected agricultural sensing systems. © 2026 IEEE.},
	type = {Conference paper},
