	abstract = {Solid-state LiDAR (SSL) technologies offer notable advantages in terms of cost, size, and durability. However, their limited field of view (FOV) and vulnerability to occlusions can hinder navigation performance when deployed as standalone sensors. Moreover, while map-based light detection and ranging (LiDAR) navigation methods enable high-accuracy positioning, they remain inherently susceptible to issues such as map gaps and outdated data. This article presents a decentralized fusion system for integrating multiple SSLs with high-accuracy 3-D digital maps to achieve robust vehicle navigation in complex urban environments. The proposed system employs a LiDAR-to-map registration (LMR) module, fused using a confidence-weighted strategy that dynamically adapts to registration quality and spatial consistency. An extended Kalman filter (EKF) further integrates inertial and odometer data to refine the navigation solution. The system's performance is validated through extensive experiments in both typical and challenging urban scenarios, including tests simulating sensor failures. Results demonstrate the effectiveness of the approach in delivering more resilient and accurate positioning to standalone SSL-based positioning under diverse challenging scenarios.  © 2001-2012 IEEE.},
