	abstract = {Visual simultaneous localization and mapping (SLAM) systems offer significant advantages in the application of augmented reality (AR)-based navigation aid on the water surface. However, their performance in aquatic environments has been relatively underexplored. In this paper, a benchmark evaluation of several visual SLAM algorithms in restricted aquatic scenarios was first conducted aiming to investigate the performance of visual SLAM in aquatic environments, where the results indicate that the stereo mode of VINS-Fusion achieves the best performance. To further improve visual SLAM systems for aquatic environments, the impact of background feature points on SLAM accuracy was studied by a background removal strategy, motivated by the observation that distant feature points and noise from the background often hinder convergence to the correct solution. However, initial attempts to remove both sky and water backgrounds simultaneously did not produce the expected improvements. Ablation studies were conducted to assess the influence of sky and water background components by removing them individually. The results demonstrate that removing only water background feature points is the most effective strategy, leading to improved accuracy and enhanced robustness. Additionally, the study reveals an important insight: although sky and water backgrounds generally degrade SLAM accuracy, they also possess potential to enhance estimation performance, particularly for rotational components, if utilized appropriately. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd},
