	abstract = {This paper describes the design, development, and validation of a high-fidelity Autonomous Mobile Robot (AMR) simulation framework built on the Robot Operating System 2 (ROS2) platform. The framework combines the Navigation2 (NAV2) and Simultaneous Localization and Mapping (SLAM) packages to provide a complete evaluation of autonomous navigation algorithms in dynamic and semi-structured environments. The simulation architecture includes workspace design, URDF/Xacro-based robot modeling, realistic physical parameterization, and seamless connection with the Gazebo physics engine. The differential-drive AMR model, outfitted with LiDAR and RGB-D sensors, was tested in a variety of scenarios, including open space, greenhouse-inspired, and warehouse environments. Experimental results show robust localization and consistent path planning, with a mean absolute trajectory error (ATE) of less than 0.1 m and an average navigation success rate of more than 93 %. Comparative performance analysis revealed that the Timed Elastic Band (TEB) planner reduced trajectory error by 25 % when compared to the Dynamic Window Approach (DWA), demonstrating superior motion smoothness and stability. The Gazebo-ROS2 synchronization maintained a real-Time factor greater than 0.95, demonstrating the framework's computational efficiency. Overall, the proposed system provides a scalable and adaptable digital twin environment for pre-deployment testing and optimization of navigation algorithms. Its modular design enables a wide range of applications, including autonomous greenhouse management, factory logistics, and sustainable smart manufacturing systems.  © 2026 IEEE.},
