	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/TIM.2026.3704243},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105043026391?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {In ultrawideband (UWB)-based indoor positioning systems, disturbances such as frequency deviation and electromagnetic interference are unavoidable. These phenomena introduce ranging errors by altering the time of arrival (TOA) measurements, and the error statistics typically vary across different base stations. Traditional positioning algorithms typically only consider ranging information while ignoring these distinct statistical characteristics. To address these limitations, a positioning algorithm is proposed that fully considers the differences among various sensors by prior information to dynamically scale and adjust measured distances. The methodology formulates positioning constraints based on 3-D geometric constructs and integrates maximum likelihood estimation (MLE) to determine optimal scaling factors for the distance measurements. Furthermore, a four-base-station configuration is employed to enhance the stability of the positioning solution. Simulation and experimental results demonstrate that this algorithm substantially reduces position estimation errors. The resulting positioning accuracy shows a marked improvement over multilateration least squares (LSs) frameworks, validating the effectiveness of the approach in mitigating the impact of ranging inaccuracies in UWB systems.  © 1963-2012 IEEE.},
	type = {Article},
	publication_stage = {Final},
