	abstract = {Accurate indoor localization remains a persistent challenge for Autonomous Mobile Robots (AMRs), particularly in industrial settings where sensor drift and environmental disturbances degrade positioning accuracy. This study proposes a cost-efficient two-layer Kalman filtering framework that enhances localization precision through multi-sensor fusion. The first layer fuses encoder and IMU measurements to refine motion estimation, while the second layer incorporates Lidar feedback to further suppress accumulated drift. The approach is validated experimentally using a Mecanum-Wheeled Mobile Robot equipped with low-cost sensors. Results demonstrate a substantial improvement in localization accuracy and robustness, with Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) reductions of over 70% compared with conventional single-sensor and single-layer filtering methods. The proposed architecture maintains stable errors below 0.05 m, confirming its suitability for cost-sensitive industrial applications. These findings suggest promising potential for further extension to dynamic and large-scale environments integrating additional sensing modalities. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2027.},
