	abstract = {Reliable yaw angle estimation in mobile robots navigating inclined agricultural terrains is a significant challenge due to terrain-induced sensor biases, motion dynamics, and the absence of absolute references like magnetometers or dual-antenna GNSS. This study introduces a novel method that combines an MPU6050 IMU and an adaptive complementary filter, whose weighting factor is optimized through the Golden Section Search (GSS) algorithm for robust sensor fusion. Unlike conventional filters, the proposed approach compensates for terrain inclination by incorporating gravitational components along inclined axes into the roll and pitch calculations, ensuring accurate correction of accelerometer readings even during dynamic motion. Numerical simulations indicate that with a convergence threshold of σ ≤ 0.146, the GSS algorithm identifies the optimal filter weighting factor within five iterations. Experimental validation using a custom-designed Autonomous Ground Vehicle (AGV) demonstrates that the optimized filter reduces yaw estimation errors by approximately 2.33° on slopes, enhancing navigation reliability without relying on magnetic or GNSS heading measurements. The AGV, measuring 25 cm in length and 32 cm in height, successfully maintains stable orientation tracking across varying terrain inclinations, showing promise for precision agriculture and other terrain-adaptive applications. This work thus contributes an effective and practical solution for maintaining yaw accuracy under conditions where traditional absolute heading sensors are unavailable or unreliable, advancing real-time navigation capabilities in challenging outdoor environments. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd},
