	abstract = {This paper investigates trajectory tracking control of networked unmanned marine vehicles under communication constraints and stochastic false data injection attacks, and develops an observer-based trajectory tracking control strategy to address quantization, scheduling, and malicious signal injection. Specifically, a nonlinear UMV model is first established, and the continuous-time dynamics are discretized via the forward Euler method. To characterize signal transmission constraints induced by the network, uniform quantization and a weighted try-once-discard (WTOD) scheduling protocol are incorporated, while stochastic FDI attacks are modeled via Bernoulli random variables characterizing their occurrence, coupled with temporally correlated attack dynamics and inter-channel coupling. Based on this framework, an observer-based controller is designed to jointly compensate for quantization effects, scheduling-induced information loss, external disturbances, and stochastic signal attacks. By leveraging stochastic analysis theory and Lyapunov stability analysis, sufficient conditions are derived to guarantee the mean-square ultimate boundedness of the closed-loop tracking system. Simulations based on both a reference trajectory and real trajectory data demonstrate that the proposed method maintains robust trajectory tracking performance in the presence of communication constraints and stochastic FDI attacks. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
