	abstract = {3D reconstruction, or mapping, plays a critical role in enabling autonomous mobile robots (AMRs) to perceive and navigate complex environments. Unlike traditional 2D methods, 3D mapping provides richer spatial context but requires the integration of multiple sensing modalities and computational models. This paper investigates the performance of 3D mapping on the LIMO ROS2 platform through sensor fusion techniques that combine LiDAR, camera, and wheel encoder data. The study compares two configurations: one using LiDAR, camera, and encoder readings, and another where LiDAR data is replaced with camera-generated point clouds. It also evaluates the quality of depth images obtained from the robot's camera against those generated by a deep learning model based on the Vision Transformer (ViT) architecture. Experimental results demonstrate that replacing LiDAR with camera-derived point clouds leads to sparser reconstructions and missing environmental features. Furthermore, the AI-based depth estimation method produces more consistent and accurate depth representations than the onboard RGB-D camera. These findings highlight the importance of sensor fusion and AI-driven depth estimation to improve the reliability and fidelity of 3D mapping for autonomous robots. © 2026 IEEE.},
