	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105042615326?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Micro-UAVs have found applications in Aerial Surveillance, Logistics, Inspection, Disaster Management among other uses. But they are often limited in their autonomous navigation of cluttered environments by the power requirements for flying as well as sensing. Active sensing techniques often require self generated energy waves to sense a scene while passive sensing techniques are often limited by their inability to perform depth calculations. Passive sensing techniques are especially appealing due to the proliferation of low cost Micro-UAVs with frontal monocular vision systems. As a result, algorithms that make use of these systems with little or no hardware modification for autonomous navigation are important and some progress has been made in literature. However, currently, most of these algorithms make use of feature based (corner and edge detection) and machine learning techniques for obstacle detection and avoidance. In this work, we derived a low computational cost statistical mechanics model that captures the natural phenomenon of haze caused by suspended particles in the atmosphere for the purposes of achieving real time 3D morphology understanding. We then derived a Model Based Predictive controller that achieves real time obstacle detection and avoidance on a low flying autonomous Micro-UAV. We validate our approach in both simulated and real world environments. © 2026 Elsevier Masson SAS.},
