	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1016/j.ins.2026.123772},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041871644?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {For a class of nonlinear uncertain systems subject to time-varying disturbances and sensor bias faults, the controller design poses significant challenges in terms of structural complexity and computational burden. Although existing schemes can ensure stability, their complex controller structures and the requirement for frequent updates hinder practical application. To overcome these limitations, this paper proposes a low-complexity fault-tolerant control scheme. First, the proposed scheme reduces the computational complexity from the exponential order of traditional designs to a polynomial order. Meanwhile, a multi filter design is employed to estimate the system states even under sensor faults, thereby enabling output feedback. Furthermore, by incorporating a quantized strategy that encodes control signals, the scheme significantly reduces both the update frequency of the control law and the required communication bandwidth. Simulations are conducted under three different bias fault conditions, and the results show that the percentage improvements in the mean absolute error (MAE) are 9.3%, 15.57% and 2.49%, respectively. Finally, compared with the continuous updating of the control law, the proposed controller allows the control law to be updated only a limited number of times. The number of updates under the three fault conditions is 702, 666 and 760, respectively. © 2026 Elsevier Inc.},
