	abstract = {In global navigation satellite system-denied underground environments, developing high-precision positioning technologies faces dual measurement challenges: magnetic aliasing effects and structural repetitiveness. Although magnetic fingerprinting provides an effective alternative, its measurement reliability is often compromised by sensor noise and environmental magnetic interference. This paper proposes multi-scale dilated dynamic time warping (MS-DDTW)-Net, an artificial intelligence-enhanced framework designed for robust two-stage positioning. The first stage employs a MS-DDTW algorithm to capture hierarchical temporal patterns, effectively mitigating positioning deviations caused by magnetometer drift. The second stage utilizes a Siamese network based on a CNN-GRU architecture for deep semantic verification, mapping measurement sequences into a discriminative metric space to resolve spatial ambiguity and suppress erroneous matching. Experimental results in real-world underground facilities demonstrate that the proposed method achieves a mean absolute error (MAE) of 3.472 m and a root mean square error of 5.771 m, outperforming traditional dynamic time warping and k-nearest neighbors methods by 44.74% and 65.39% in terms of MAE, respectively. This hybrid framework significantly enhances measurement reliability and positioning robustness, offering a novel technical solution for localization in complex underground environments. © 2026 IOP Publishing Ltd. All rights, including for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies, are reserved. This article is available under the terms of the IOP-Standard License.},
