	abstract = {The bias of a whole-angle vibratory gyroscope is not a stationary offset but an angle-dependent waveform shaped by structural errors, circuit-induced control and detection errors, and thermally driven operating variations, whose coupled effects are represented in the present observation model by effective Fourier bias components rather than separated into source-specific parameters. Existing bias models usually rely on low-order angular harmonics, especially the (Formula presented) and (Formula presented) components, which limits their ability to represent high-order angle-dependent bias structures. This study proposes a High-Order Fourier-Basis Long Short-Term Memory Physics-Informed Neural Network (LSTM-PINN) framework for angle-dependent bias waveform reconstruction and harmonic component characterization. High-order potential and Rayleigh dissipation functionals are retained up to the eighth order, from which a (Formula presented) even-order Fourier harmonic basis is derived. This Lagrangian-derived harmonic structure defines the Fourier-basis analytical decoder of the LSTM-PINN solver. Instead of treating the angle-dependent bias waveform as an unconstrained regression target, the solver infers the scale-factor term and Fourier bias components from historical sequences of the amplitude-control envelope, quadrature-control envelope, and resonant frequency, and reconstructs the angle-dependent bias waveform through the Lagrangian-derived analytical decoder. Experiments on 16-electrode micro-shell gyroscopes show stable reconstruction, with a mean RMSE of (Formula presented) deg/s and a mean R<sup>2</sup> of 0.9886. Architecture ablations show that both the PINN and the LSTM are necessary: relative to the black-box LSTM baseline and the MLP-PINN baseline, the full LSTM-PINN reduces the 1 Hz mean RMSE by 90.5% and 59.2%, respectively. Expanding the physically admissible basis from (Formula presented) to (Formula presented) reduces the across-label mean RMSE by 32.0%, while repeated-segment tests show quasi-stationary inferred high-order components. Over a seven-day evaluation horizon at (Formula presented) and (Formula presented), the Day-7 across-rate mean RMSE and R<sup>2</sup> were (Formula presented) and 0.9857, respectively. Within the validated micro-shell device and laboratory operating range, these results support the proposed framework as a device-physics-constrained temporal method for angle-dependent bias waveform reconstruction. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
