	abstract = {This paper addresses the problem of electromagnetic detumbling for non-cooperative space targets in open-world scenarios, where the target's dynamic parameters (e.g., inertia tensor, magnetic tensor) and motion states are unknown or partially known. We propose an integrated framework combining robust state estimation and adaptive control to achieve effective angular momentum dissipation. The system dynamics are derived based on the magnetic tensor model, extended to non-uniform fields via an effective factor correction. An extended Kalman filter (EKF) is designed to simultaneously estimate the target's angular velocity, attitude, and unknown parameters from noisy measurements (e.g., vision-based relative pose and magnetometer readings). Based on the estimated state, an adaptive sliding mode control law is developed to regulate the magnetic field generated by the servicer spacecraft, ensuring fast convergence of angular velocity despite parameter uncertainties. Numerical simulations under various open-world conditions (e.g., different initial angular velocities, unknown material properties, and varying relative poses) demonstrate the effectiveness and robustness of the proposed approach. Ground experiments using a Helmholtz coil validate the underlying magnetic tensor model with errors below 2.2%, supporting the fidelity of the simulation. The results highlight the potential of integrating state estimation and adaptive control for safe and efficient space debris removal in uncertain environments. © 2026 IEEE.},
