	abstract = {This study proposed a multi-objective trajectory optimisation method for space dual-arm robots utilising stacked integrated learning to address the optimal trajectory planning challenge of capturing a non-cooperative tumbling target exhibiting both spin and nutation in orbit. First, the screw method was applied to describe the global motion of the space dual-arm robot to circumvent the complexity of the D–H parameter method in establishing a coordinate system. Second, extreme learning machine (ELM), long short-term memory network (LSTM), and least squares support vector machine (LSSVM) models were integrated using the stacking integrated learning method to enhance the predictive accuracy of the motion state of non-cooperative targets. Trajectory planning was subdivided into three phases: rapid localisation, synchronous approach, and tracking retention. Starting from the robot model, the positioning accuracy of the end-effector and minimisation of the base disturbance were considered as the optimisation objectives, and constraints such as the robot joint angles, velocities, and motion times were considered. Further, the penalty mechanism for collision detection was introduced. The joint trajectory curve was smoothed by parameterising it with a fifth-order polynomial. Subsequently, the trajectory planning challenges in each phase were converted into a succession of multi-objective optimisation problems, which were ultimately resolved using the sparrow search algorithm (SSA) to achieve optimal trajectory planning. Simulation results demonstrated that the proposed method effectively mitigated base disturbances while ensuring end-effector positioning accuracy. © 2024 COSPAR},
