	abstract = {Conventional frame-based visual simultaneous localization and mapping (SLAM) suffers from high-speed, poor lighting, and motion blur challenges. The combination of event cameras fused with IMU and wheel speed can offer a new path towards robust localization. The current study introduces a new Hierarchical Diffusion Graph Fusion SLAM (HDG-Fusion-SLAM) model, and provides innovative contributions to the initial tightlycoupled visual-inertial-wheel speed framework by adding a dual layer spatiotemporal calibration module, and a graph neural diffusion estimator. In the front end, an event diffusion encoder achieves temporal alignment of asynchronous event streams; the intermediate layer consists of a multimodal graph attention network to fuse IMU pre-integration and wheel speed constraints, with dynamically allocated weights; and the back end applies a hierarchical optimization scheme that calibrates coarse spatiotemporal states to prevent drift, then factor-graph optimizes for refined joint estimation of trajectory and external parameters. The resulting design guarantees spatiotemporal coherence between modalities, and is robust in wheel slip and in noisy environments. Empirical evaluations suggest that HDG-FusionSLAM shows a greater than 25 percent increase in trajectory accuracy over existing baselines. © 2025 IEEE.},
