	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041738879?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {This paper presents a comprehensive, cost-effective ROS-based educational platform designed for autonomous navigation with multi-sensor fusion capabilities. The system integrates 360° LIDAR, IMU, ultrasonic sensors, wheel encoders, and a vision module, all managed through the complete ROS navigation stack. Key technical features include Simultaneous Localization and Mapping (SLAM) via Gmapping, robust localization through Adaptive Monte Carlo Localization (AMCL), and real-time obstacle avoidance using the Dynamic Window Approach (DWA). The platform supports progressive learning through modular experiments, ranging from basic teleoperation and sensor data visualization to full autonomous navigation capabilities. Extensive validation through real-world experiments demonstrates effectiveness across diverse scenarios including human following, staircase detection, geometric path execution, and environmental surveillance. Performance analysis reveals ±1m mapping accuracy, 89% obstacle avoidance success rate in dynamic environments, and consistent system latency below 100ms. Multi-sensor fusion achieves 89% synchronization accuracy with drift maintained below 5ms between LIDAR, IMU, and encoder data streams. This platform provides a scalable, open-source solution for robotics education, enabling resource-constrained institutions to deliver comprehensive hands-on training in autonomous systems while effectively bridging theoretical concepts with practical implementation. © 2025 IEEE.},
