	abstract = {Robust localization under environmental variations is a critical function for the successful operation and navigation of planetary rovers. This paper assumes robust rover operation under a slippery environment and a few distinguishable features. To deal with the issues in that environment, the authors propose utilizing sensor fusion techniques to integrate multiple localization results. SLAM (Simultaneous Localization and Mapping) utilizing RGB and infrared depth sensors is widely applied to commercial automobiles, and has the possibility of failing localization because of few distinguishable features. Therefore, the authors propose to combine other ranging data with the SLAM data. The authors propose to utilize the integrated results of two types of data: wheel odometry and radio ranging system. Wheel odometry integrates wheels rotation measurement, and has the potential to fail because of wheel slippage. Radio ranging can get ranging data independent of the environmental conditions when communication is established, but the accuracy changes by the coordinates of transmitter and receiver devices. This paper proposes data fusion of SLAM, wheel odometry, and radio ranging to achieve robust localization. This paper proposes the effective verification. First, this paper authors confirm the performance and function of single component systems. Second, they verify data fusion method. Finally, they verify the full system. We propose to extract the experimental components and to confirm the experiment process. They set high-priority tasks for confirmation as a maximum number of matching features of visual SLAM parameter and data fusion accuracy variation between the single sensor and multiple sensors data fusion and showed the effectiveness of parameter setting and proposed method. This scheme must be useful for further discussion of the localization rover system. Copyright © 2025 by the International Astronautical Federation (IAF). All rights reserved.},
