	doi = {10.1109/TIM.2025.3626109},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105020982418?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Nowadays, microelectromechanical systems (MEMS) inertial measurement units (IMUs) have gained significant attention in both academia and industry areas due to the advanced development in circuit manufacturing technology. Benefiting from these technological advancements, it is natural to fuse multiple low-cost and miniaturized MEMS–IMUs to overcome the error accumulation of a single IMU. However, existing methods generally rely on known spatiotemporal parameters or specific assumptions. To fill this up, we propose a real-time attitude estimation method termed MI-Odometry that only uses randomly distributed IMUs. Meanwhile, a coarse-to-fine online calibration approach is also proposed to eliminate the need for exhaustive offline spatiotemporal calibration without using any prior knowledge. The proposed method is extensively evaluated in both simulation and real-world experiments to validate its effectiveness and superiority over state-of-the-art methods. The source code is available at https://github.com/GREAT-WHU/MI-Odom to benefit the research community. © 2025 IEEE. All rights reserved,},
