	abstract = {Achieving precise real-time trajectory control for high degree-of-freedom (DoF) robotic manipulators is challenging due to system uncertainties and exogenous disturbances, such as sensor noise and actuator failures. These factors often hinder accurate state estimation and disrupt control mechanisms. This paper proposes an integrated control approach using an attention-based Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU) for inverse system identification, paired with nonlinear model predictive control (NMPC) utilizing an augmented state estimation scheme. The attention-based GRU accurately models the complex inverse dynamics of the manipulator, capturing time dependencies in the system's motion. Once validated, the GRU inversion model is cascaded with the robot manipulator, and NMPC is applied for real-time control. An event-driven observer mechanism within NMPC further enhances estimation accuracy, updating internal states as needed to account for unexpected disturbances. Experimental results confirm that this method significantly enhances both modeling accuracy and operational speed while maintaining low computational requirements. The proposed approach demonstrates robust performance in high-precision trajectory control, addressing the challenges of traditional NMPC by providing resilience to uncertainties and improving system stability. © 2024 Elsevier Ltd},
