	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034460114?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Accurate and robust localization for Automated Guided Vehicles (AGVs) in dynamic port environments is a critical yet challenging task due to severe global positioning system (GPS) multipath effects and perceptual degeneracy. To address these issues, this paper presents TIDE-LIO, a novel, tightly-coupled LiDAR-inertial localization system based on a hybrid filter-graph optimization framework. The system features a robust Iterated Error-State Kalman Filter (IESKF) frontend that generates high-frequency, locally precise odometry. The backend employs a factor graph to ensure global consistency and eliminate long-term drift. This graph fuses relative motion estimates with absolute measurements from multiple sources, including validated GPS, scan-to-map matching, and loop closures. In this paper, a three-layer validation mechanism is presented that effectively identifies and rejects GPS outliers caused by multipath, enabling reliable global correction. Extensive evaluations on a public dataset demonstrate that TIDE-LIO provides a robust and reliable localization solution. The system successfully ensures global accuracy while maintaining high local precision, proving its effectiveness in challenging port environments. ©2025 IEEE.},
