	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1016/j.measurement.2025.116907},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85216925320?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Describing road environments and vehicle motion with a unified dynamic model is challenging due to their unstructured nature and variability. Traditional Kalman filter-based navigation algorithms adapt to actual motion states, but this adaptation limits the accuracy and stability of integrated navigation systems. To address these challenges, this paper proposes an adaptive Kalman filter (AKF)-based integrated navigation method enhanced by convolutional neural networks and gated recurrent units (CNN-GRU). The AKF processes inertial sensor data, while the CNN-GRU improves the estimation of self-motion uncertainty. The identified motion state is used to dynamically adjust the noise covariances Q and R, with their relationship to the motion state m determined through correlation analysis. Experimental results validate the proposed method, demonstrating significant reductions in position and velocity errors compared to traditional Kalman filter algorithms. These findings confirm the method's effectiveness in improving the accuracy and stability of integrated navigation systems under varying vehicle motion conditions. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd},
