	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1016/j.measurement.2024.116412},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85212044932?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Inertial navigation system (INS) indoor position technology is receiving increasing attention from researchers, especially the combination of inertial navigation and Ultra-wideband (UWB), which can achieve good results in indoor positioning. This article further explores and improves the positioning accuracy based on this model. This article proposes an optimization approach to address the issues of loose coupling in traditional inertial navigation, utilizing deep learning and improved particle filter to improve its accuracy. Firstly, we use an adaptive threshold gait detection algorithm and replaced traditional extended Kalman filter (EKF) with adaptive extended Kalman filter. Secondly, in terms of UWB data processing, compensation is provided for non-line-of-sight (NLOS) UWB data. Finally, the correct measurement values obtained from inertial navigation are fed into the particle filter improved by the firefly algorithm (FAPF) for final positioning. At the same time, we can note that the traditional use of inertial navigation to calculate the heading and step length to guide the motion of each particle in the particle filter has a significant problem, which is that the cumulative error is becoming increasingly large. Therefore, this article uses recurrent neural network (RNN) to regress three-axis acceleration and three-axis angular velocity to obtain step length and heading, in order to guide the motion of each particle. Through the final experiment, we obtained a positioning error of around 0.4 m. © 2024 Elsevier Ltd},
