	abstract = {This paper presents an adaptive Kalman filtering localization system that integrates GPS, IMU, and wheel encoder data to improve the positioning accuracy and robustness of wheeled mobile robots in complex environments. The system employs a nine-dimensional state vector to model position, velocity, attitude, and acceleration, with a Kalman filter framework for real-time state estimation. To address intermittent sensor signal loss, a covariance adaptation mechanism and dynamic gain adjustment are introduced, enhancing stability and reliability under weak or obstructed signals. Experimental evaluations under normal operation, GPS loss, IMU drift, and wheel speed error show that the proposed method achieves average positioning errors of 1.25 m, 2.05 m, 1.72 m, and 1.45 m respectively, compared to 2.98 m, 3.47 m, 3.01 m, and 2.65 m using a traditional Kalman filter. Furthermore, the system maintains error bounds within 1 m over 1 minute and 4 m over 4 minutes during GPS outages. These results demonstrate superior trajectory tracking and error convergence, outperforming fixed-parameter filters and offering strong adaptability for long-term autonomous navigation in dynamic environments. ©2025 IEEE.},
