	pages = {962 - 967},
	doi = {10.1145/3716368.3735266},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105017799184?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Autonomous navigation and space-mapping methodologies in Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) typically depend on advanced, resource-intensive technologies such as LiDAR, GPS, and inertial measurement units (IMUs). These systems provide high-precision navigation and mapping capabilities but at the cost of increasing UAV platforms' complexity, power consumption, and unit cost. As a result, deploying UAVs in swarms, essential for large-scale applications such as environmental monitoring, disaster response, and agricultural surveillance, becomes economically non-viable. To address this challenge, we present VisNAV, a vision-assisted navigation and mapping framework designed to provide efficient and cost-effective solutions for UAV-based agricultural field exploration without auxiliary sensor support. VisNAV aims to optimize the overall performance of the UAV's navigation while reducing the reliance on expensive hardware, making it more suitable for broader, scalable applications. Our proposed model is based on focal length optimization and is tailored for agricultural applications, providing visual accuracy of 12-14 cm. It is designed to perform effectively across various agricultural terrains. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
