	doi = {10.1109/ICCIT68739.2025.11491493},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041648219?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Anomalies within robotic applications can arise from hardware faults, environmental changes or unexpected operational deviations. Current research in this field has typically employed single-sensor monitoring or computationally intensive deep learning approaches, limiting the scalability of resourceconstrained low-power embedded systems in the industrial arena. To provide a more efficient and affordable solution in anomalous system detection, this study propose a multi-sensor fusion framework that combines multiple data streams like current, vibration, and temperature modalities with supervised machine learning models. The experimental dataset was collected from a 4DOF hobby servo-based robotic manipulator, where the proposed models were trained and validated for anomaly classification. A comparative evaluation of multiple classifiers is conducted, where the Random Forest (rf) model achieves the best performance with 98% accuracy and an f1-score of 97. The experimental results demonstrate that the proposed approach provides a balance between computational complexity and detection reliability, making it well-suited for real-time application in industrial robotic systems. © 2025 IEEE.},
