	year = {2025},
	journal = {SAFEPROCESS 2025 - 14th CAA Symposium on Fault Detection, Supervision, and Safety for Technical Processes},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/SAFEPROCESS67117.2025.11267819},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105031113479?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Indoor poultry farms rely on inspection robots to sustain precise environmental control, a factor essential for limiting rapid disease transmission and large-scale bird losses. Yet the challenging conditions inside these facilities - marked by harsh lighting contrasts and frequent water accumulation - introduce major obstacles. Conventional navigation strategies that depend on a single sensor often struggle in such settings, leading to problems like laser drift and errors in visual guide-line extraction. To address these issues, we introduce a new hybrid navigation approach that combines laser and vision sensing. This method dynamically calculates a fused yaw angle based on the real-time reliability of each sensing modality, thereby removing the need for physical navigation lines. Tests conducted in operational poultry houses show that our approach not only mitigates the fundamental weaknesses of single-sensor systems but also substantially improves navigation accuracy and overall efficiency. Consequently, it offers a promising pathway for enhancing inspection robot performance in complex indoor poultry farming environments.  © 2025 IEEE.},
