	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85210809087?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {With the rapid development of UAVs, sensors, artificial intelligence technologies and control theories in recent years, autonomous indoor UAV flight still faces many challenges. The application of a single sensor is often constrained by its own limitations, which makes the stable positioning and flight of UAVs in complex indoor environments extremely difficult. To solve this problem, this paper develops an indoor autonomous flight obstacle avoidance system by combining a binocular camera with a 2D LiDAR sensor and correcting the UAV state by fusing flight control data. The experimental results show that the system successfully realises real-time positioning and map building in an unknown indoor environment, and at the same time is able to achieve one-key take-off and precise hovering of the quadcopter UAV. This not only provides an effective way to overcome sensor limitations, but also offers a completely new solution for the application of UAVs in complex indoor environments. © 2024 IEEE.},
