	abstract = {This paper proposes a method for estimating angular velocity without using gyroscope. The approach is based on Micro-Electro-Mechanical System (MEMS) accelerometer and magnetometer, and it addresses the limitation of traditional gyroscope in low-cost and compact robotic systems. By eliminating the reliance on physical gyroscopes, the proposed method reduces system cost and power consumption while improving the accuracy and robustness of angular velocity estimation, thereby meeting the requirements of industrial applications for attitude sensing and control. The gyroless angular velocity estimation method initially filters high-frequency noise in the raw data from the accelerometer. Then it adjusts dynamic weighting factors through norm fault detection. Subsequently, the output vectors of sensors are fused to construct a rotation matrix, and angular velocity is estimated using quaternion differentiation. The experimental results demonstrate that the proposed method which achieves a root mean square error (RMSE) of 0.9861°/s, outperforms the Tait-Bryan angle (TBA) method both low-speed and high-speed dynamic conditions, exhibiting notable improvements in accuracy and robustness. These results illustrate the efficacy of the proposed method and its potential applications in robotic systems.  © 2024 IEEE.},
