	doi = {10.1109/ICCEIC60201.2023.10426652},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85186538710?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Simultaneous Localization and Mapping (SLAM) is primarily divided into two components: the front-end odometry and the back-end optimization. During the measurement process, errors in feature extraction and noise effects lead to an accumulation of discrepancies between the estimated position by the front-end odometry and the actual position over time. Therefore, loop closure detection is introduced for back-end optimization. It corrects accumulated errors by identifying previously visited landmarks or positions in the scene. This paper builds upon the LeGO-LOAM-SC SLAM algorithm, which combines scan context and lightweight ground optimization LiDAR Odometry and Mapping (LeGO-LOAM). We integrate point cloud intensity information into the global descriptor to mitigate feature loss and consequent misalignment caused by the use of a single descriptor. Loop closure detection is performed using the fused global descriptor. When a loop frame is detected, the relative pose between the current frame and the history is added as a constraint to GTSAM for graphical optimization and point cloud map construction. Experiments conducted on the publicly available KITTI dataset's sequence 05 demonstrate that the proposed enhanced algorithm outperforms existing methods in terms of performance. © 2023 IEEE.},
