	abstract = {Video navigation looks like a promising means of UAV control, especially in autonomous flight in GPS denied environment. However, just the simple addition of the onboard camera to the INS system often does not bring tangible effects in the absence of means of recognition and interpretation of the video image. That is why methods of extracting navigation information from a sequence of video images play a crucial role. Known approaches use a comparison of the current image with the characteristic objects on the ground preloaded into the memory of the control system, but it is necessary to remember about the change in the recorded image depending on the season, time of day, observation angle, etc. Therefore, the features of the observed objects which are invariant to the above-mentioned variations in observation conditions, such as the direction of roads or power lines, the profile of the coastline of water bodies, the orientation of large buildings, etc., are very important. In this paper, we are focusing on the UAV position during landing. As an example, we consider the results of our experiments and evaluate the accuracy level of the position estimation when integrating video surveillance with data from INS. © 2022 IACAS 2022 - 61st Israel Annual Conference on Aerospace Science. All rights reserved.},
