	abstract = {A Simultaneous Location and Mapping (SLAM) solution tackles several challenges to correctly map an unknown environment. Visual SLAM (VSLAM) make use of stereoscopy computer vision technique to calculate Visual Odometry and improve map accuracy. This paper proposes a low cost VSLAM prototype using webcams and an Android smartphone, demonstrating its viability. The image capture is software synchronized by using multiple semaphore controlled threads, while an Android application queries the smartphone's sensors at the highest possible pooling rate. The Visual Odometry is calculated on the synchronized frames and fused with the smartphone's sensors to estimate the robot's pose. Three different datasets were gathered and analyzed off-line. The approach presents a Loop Closure Error of 1.6% to 2.3%, achieving similar results obtained by hardware synchronized cameras. Our analysis showed that the smartphone's GPS was unreliable on low speed scenarios and the dataset processing was computationally expensive, making it suitable for applications where cost, instead of performance, is a crucial factor. © 2018 ACM.},
